%% gsn_boot.glp --- the app of GSN, the grassroots social network, on two smartphones (GSG paper, Sections 2 and 6, "The Grassroots Super-App" and "Mini-Apps on the Prototype"), as gsg_boot.glp runs GSG's app: under the boot harness, one isolate per person, each with its own key pair, running the social graph of programs/social/graph/core as its super-app and hosting GSN's mini-app (programs/apps/gsn).  The program is graph/core, named as :boot's second argument, and this file is loaded on top of it, with the boot clause and the persons; the smartphone, gsn_phone/4, is graph/core's (core/gsn_phone.glp), started by core's agent/2 on the person's first act (IGLP 0e1c9e0, Implementation Notes, "Boot"):
%%   :artefact programs/apps/gsn programs/social/graph/core
%%   :boot programs/apps/gsn_boot.glp programs/social/graph/core
%%
%% Each person trusts the compiler's key, reads the certified artefact gsn.glpw from graph/core with load_file/2 and installs it, with one end of a person channel; alice connects to bob, they befriend in the super-app's social graph across the isolates, alice invites bob to the mini-app, bob consents, and the handshake opens a root channel between their two executions.  Over that conversation the mini-app runs GSN's contract, as play_network of programs/apps/gsn runs it: each establishes their identity (GKK:1), they come to know each other (GKK:2) and become friends (GSG:1), alice creates the group friends, adds bob to it and sends him the message 'Hi Bob', bob sends the group the message 'Hi all', which alice delivers to him, and bob leaves the group.  The other end of each person channel is the mini-app's own scripted person (programs/apps/gsn/person.glp), activated from the module value under its role; its screen goes to the person, one line each, and so does what the super-app told the person of the friendship in its own social graph, sg(...).
%%
%% The boot settles, as gsg_boot.glp's does: the boot harness reduces each event until the program is quiescent, as the device runtime does (IGLP, Implementation Notes: "On each event the agent binds what the event delivers, reduces until quiescent"), and after each event the platform's agent takes its own transactions until none holds and then waits for the next (vGLP, Section 5.4, "Triggers").  Section APPS of the suite runs this file and holds each person's lines, in order.

procedure boot.
boot :-
    agent(ch(_?, _), ch(_?, _))@alice,
    say(alice, alice, [bob], ch(_?, _))@alice,
    agent(ch(_?, _), ch(_?, _))@bob,
    say(bob, bob, [alice], ch(_?, _))@bob.

%% Every agent's initial goal is agent(ch(_?,_),ch(_?,_)), the person channel first and the network channel second (GLP-Spec maglp.tex, Definition "Multiagent GLP"), which the program, graph/core, answers: a boot defines no agent/2, and the person goal's first act names the entry the program starts (IGLP 0e1c9e0, Implementation Notes, "Boot").  The person scripted at each agent, say/4, run on its person channel in the person's place, tells the agent the entry, gsn_phone/4 (graph/core/gsn_phone.glp), with the person's part in the script, their own key and the keys of the peers they know, its ground arguments, the readable names being the atoms that stand for the keys in a simulation ("An agent learns the keys of its peers from its person"; Remark "Agent Names"), and reads nothing.
Role     ::= alice ; bob.
SayAct ::= gsn_phone(Role, Key, List(Key)).
SayIn  ::= [SayAct | SayEnd].
SayEnd ::= [].

exported procedure say(Role?, Key?, List(Key)?, Channel(Stream(Constant), SayIn)?).
say(R, Me, Ps, ch(_, [gsn_phone(R?, Me?, Ps?)])).
